# 5. Experimental Evaluation Metrics & Infrastructure

## 5.1 Causal Funnel Progression Metrics

Rather than evaluating policy performance using a single scalar win rate, HEIST evaluates policies along the sequential causal chain:

1. **Scout Tag Rate ($ P_{\text{tag}} $):** $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{len}(\text{tagged\_pois}) > 0) \right] $
2. **Terminal Disable Rate ($ P_{\text{term}} $):** $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{terminal\_disabled}) \right] $
3. **Loot Acquisition Rate ($ P_{\text{loot}} $):** $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{loot\_acquired}) \right] $
4. **Extraction Call Rate ($ P_{\text{ext}} $):** $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{extraction\_triggered}) \right] $
5. **Team Win Rate ($ P_{\text{win}} $):** $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{win}) \right] $

A model suffers from *Causal Bottlenecking* at stage $ m $ if $ P_m > 0 $ but $ P_{m+1} \approx 0 $.

## 5.2 Credit Attribution Index (CAI)

To quantify how effectively an algorithm attributes credit to upstream enablers versus downstream extractors, we define the **Credit Attribution Index (CAI)** for agent $ i $:

$ \text{CAI}_i = \text{Corr}\left( \sum_{t=1}^T r_{t,i}^{\text{shaped}}, \quad Y_{\text{terminal}} \right) $

where $ Y_{\text{terminal}} \in \{+15, -10\} $ is the episode's shared outcome. High positive $ \text{CAI}_i $ for Scout and Hacker indicates that enabling contributions are correctly correlated with team success.

## 5.3 Loss Mode Cause Attribution

Episode failures are classified into three disjoint loss modes using multiline TeX case math:

$$$
\text{Loss Cause} =
\begin{cases}
    \text{alarm\_max} & \text{if } A_t \ge 100.0 \\
    \text{guard\_catch} & \text{if } \exists i, j: \text{Manhattan}(p_i, g_j) \le d_{\text{catch}} \\
    \text{timeout} & \text{if } t \ge T_{\text{max}}
\end{cases}
$$$

## 5.4 Hardware Protection Kill Switch (`tools/thermal_guard.py`)

To prevent thermal degradation during long multi-stage campaign runs across all evaluated models, HEIST executes live thermal monitoring at every iteration:

- **CPU Threshold:** $ T_{\text{CPU}} < 85.0^\circ\text{C} $ (derived from Intel/AMD TjMax safety limits).
- **GPU Threshold:** $ T_{\text{GPU}} < 83.0^\circ\text{C} $ (derived from NVIDIA GPU boost thermal throttling limits).

If temperature exceeds limits, `thermal_guard.py` triggers an emergency `pkill` to gracefully stop python processes and protect hardware integrity.

## 5.5 Experimental Results: The Sparsity Wall

The empirical evaluation of flat MARL models across the HEIST curriculum reveals a fundamental limitation in temporal credit assignment. While models like MAPPO, COMA, and our advanced causal variants (MAPPO-CAR, MARC) demonstrate learning capacity on dense, small maps, they consistently hit a "Sparsity Wall" as the curriculum scales to the large sparse maps in Stage 4 ($ 50 \times 50 $ grid).

In these large sparse environments, the time-delay between causal dependencies becomes exceedingly large. Consequently, the flat Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) signal decays to zero before upstream enabling actions can be properly credited. This causes the value gradient to collapse, forcing the models into exploration failure and resulting in a 0.000 win rate across all baselines in Stage 4.

