## 5.6 Conclusion: Hierarchical MARL (H-MARL)

To overcome the Sparsity Wall induced by flat temporal credit decay, we conclude by proposing Hierarchical MARL (H-MARL) as the requisite structural solution. By abstracting extended temporal dependencies into high-level macro-actions or subgoals, H-MARL allows credit to bypass the low-level step-by-step decay of GAE.

Specifically, we propose the **CHARM** architecture (Causal Hierarchical Advantage Routing Multi-agent). By isolating spatial and temporal dependencies into a two-tiered hierarchy, CHARM structurally bridges the causal delay between upstream enablers and downstream outcomes. We benchmark CHARM against state-of-the-art hierarchical baselines including MAHIRO, ROMA, and LRS, demonstrating that only a fully hierarchical causal approach can reliably solve the Stage 4 facility.
